INT. CASINO COUNTING ROOM - NIGHT

Stacked cash. Camera domes. A hum of machines.

ELIAS VANDERKOOI, fifty, in a grey suit, sits at a desk and sorts bills with a licked thumb. He does not look up when the vault door clicks.

His partner MARISOL FONTANA, forty, closes it behind her and leans against it. She holds a duffel bag by one strap. She does not look at the money. She looks at him.

ELIAS
Late.

MARISOL
Traffic.

He sets down the bills. She lets the duffel slide to the floor and kicks it across the carpet. It stops against his shoe.

ELIAS
That's the whole amount?

MARISOL
It's all of it.

Elias sighs. He stands, slowly, and he unbuttons his jacket.

A third figure steps out from behind the shelving. DETECTIVE HARLAN BECKMANN, sixty, in a trench coat, holds a badge in one hand and handcuffs in the other.

BECKMANN
Evening.

Marisol closes her eyes. Elias lifts his hands, calm, almost relieved, and says nothing at all.
